.TH CVEC_AT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_at \- access element at index (type-inferred, returns reference)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

element_ref = cvec_at(v, index);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-inferred wrapper for cvector_at(). It gives a reference to the
element, already of the right type, rather than a pointer, so you can use
it as an lvalue in an assignment.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v      Vector to access
index  Zero-based index of the element
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A reference to the element at the index.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
What you get is a reference, not a pointer, and you can put it on the
left side of an assignment.

index is evaluated exactly once, so an expression with a side effect, for
example cvec_at(v, i++), is safe to pass.

An index that is out of bounds stops the program with _ccol_fatal_err().
If you'd rather get NULL for such an index, use cvec_at_ptr.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_push(vec, 42);
int val = cvec_at(vec, 0);  // val == 42
cvec_at(vec, 0) = 5;        // the first element is now 5
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_at (3),
.BR cvec_at_ptr (3)
